     "title": "Family Cohesion",
     "group": null,
     "align": [
      "cohesion",
      "life"
     ],
     "intro": [
      "QF is where social and family bonds are reinforced through programs and activities that aim to build stronger connections, enhance mutual understanding, and preserve traditions while catering to new and evolving challenges in our daily lives."
     ],
     "items": [
      "<strong>Qatar Reads’ Family Reading Program</strong> is helping build a new generation of learners who love to read and spend quality time with their families. QNL’s Qatar Reads Family Reading Program reached more than 1,700 children, while school engagement scaled significantly after alignment with the Ministry of Education and Higher Education, leading to a threefold increase in participation through the School Membership Program (with over 200 schools registered as of 2025). Reading was further embedded into professional development through <em>Read to Lead</em>, adopted by the Ministry of Communications and Information Technology, and received 95% positive feedback, demonstrating the program’s relevance across both educational and professional contexts.",
      "QF additionally supports parents in delivering their duties, including raising their children to nurture all aspects of their developmental journey. QF raises the importance of family in progressing our society through education, family policy advocacy, and evidence-based programming. QF supports family engagement for children with special needs, including children with autism. Parent and caregiver training is offered through our PUE schools or through The Learning Center. In addition, through Renad Academy’s Early Bird Program, more than 100 parents or caregivers were trained to provide better support to their children’s learning needs.",
      "QF further shaped national policies to strengthen family wellbeing and protect Qatar’s youth. This year, five policy recommendations by the <strong>Doha International Family Institute</strong> (DIFI) had evidence of implementation by national stakeholders. These included the ban of Roblox, the establishment of a Digital Safety Committee for children and youth, the introduction of a family curriculum piloted in public schools, and new provisions in the Civil Human Resources Law such as a marriage incentive and extended maternity leave.",
      "DIFI continued to strengthen the evidence base for family wellbeing and social policy in Qatar through three key publications in 2026. These included a cost-benefit analysis of QF’s work-life balance policies, which found that Flexible Work Arrangements contributed to improved wellbeing, reduced psychological distress, and higher job satisfaction; a national survey on family cohesion among Qatari families, published with SESRI at Qatar University; and a study with the National Planning Council on fertility trends, which identified policy options related to flexible work, parental leave, childcare, and financial support for marriage and childbearing.",
      "The launch of the <strong>Parenting Center \"Kanaf\"</strong> by the Ministry of Social Development and the Family on 14 April 2026 exemplifies a successful research‑to‑policy pipeline, translating evidence and advocacy into institutional action. Referenced during the inauguration, DIFI’s research played a role in shaping the Center’s design and purpose. Beyond service delivery, Kanaf serves as a national platform that bridges scientific knowledge with institutional practice, contributing to the development of an integrated model for parental support across different stages of family life."
     ],
     "id": "d-family-cohesion",
     "img": "v4/img/stories/social-progress-04-herfah.jpg",
     "stat": [
      "5",
      "DIFI policy recommendations put into practice nationally"
     ]
